<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Cleartext HTTP is permitted on purpose: Seekarr talks to instances the user
    runs themselves, and a plain `http://192.168.1.10:8080` LAN address is a
    normal, supported setup.

    It cannot be narrowed here. Android's `domain` matching works on DNS name
    suffixes, so RFC-1918 ranges (192.168/16, 10/8, 172.16/12) are not
    expressible — listing them one address at a time is not workable.

    The restriction therefore lives in the app, where the host is actually
    known: UrlUtils.cleartextWarning() flags an `http://` URL pointing at a
    non-private host in onboarding and in the service settings form, so the user
    is warned before credentials would travel in the clear over the internet.

    Note the deliberate asymmetry with Apple platforms: ios/macos Info.plist set
    only NSAllowsLocalNetworking (no NSAllowsArbitraryLoads), so there the OS
    itself blocks arbitrary cleartext.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system"/>
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
